WRENCH RANGE PROBLEM — 23/08/2026. The wrench's minimum is 1Nm. Rails have now
been loosened to significantly below that just by feel, i.e. the actual sweet
spot is BELOW what this wrench can measure at all. Do not conclude "poor
quality rails" from this alone — MGN12H mounts on M3 screws, and M3-into-
aluminium-extrusion is commonly spec'd well under 1Nm in general assembly
guidance (often 0.4-0.8Nm, sometimes lower into a T-nut), specifically to
avoid crushing the softer aluminium thread. Both rails needing sub-1Nm to stop
ringing is equally consistent with "this is just the correct torque for this
fastener/material and the wrench bought doesn't cover it" as with any rail
defect — a one-off bad part would not normally affect both rails identically.
NEXT: get a wrench/driver rated for the actual range in play (precision drivers
for this kind of fastener commonly go down to ~0.05-0.3Nm), rather than
concluding the hardware needs replacing on this evidence alone.

METHOD WARNINGS LEARNED THE HARD WAY
  - Single captures have ~15% run-to-run scatter. Three runs minimum before
    trusting any modest effect. An apparent I1000->I500 improvement vanished
    entirely once repeats were run.
  - M569.1 with parameters DROPS DRIVERS TO OPEN LOOP. Re-home after, and
    verify with M569 Pxx.0 (not M569.1, which reports PID but not mode).
  - Resonance-lab plugin sweep is quantisation-limited above ~100Hz: at 80
    steps/mm and its 60mm/s^2-per-Hz excitation, 135Hz = 2.2 microsteps of
    commanded motion. Cannot usefully probe 204Hz. Use decel ring-down instead.

