Trev's read on the whole day, and it fits: clamping a straight rail against a
mounting surface that isn't true TWISTS THE CARRIAGE, and that twist is what
produced everything seen today - no torque sweet spot, "by feel" unreproducible
between two identical attempts, amplitude-dependent onset, and the front
reading that showed up once and vanished on retest. The fix is correcting the
actual geometry (shimming), not searching for a torque number - torque was
never the causal variable, it was just how hard the twist got squeezed in.

Front-right fastener was deliberately slackened + axis moved + retightened
earlier today as its own small test; Trev suspects it may not have gone back
fully tight, which would explain the flickering front reading directly (a
loose screw gives inconsistency, not a stable defect signature).

IMPORTANT DISTINCTION recorded here for next time: the original static twist
survey (frame_survey_20260821.txt section 2) zeroed its measurement AT
fastener 1 (front) - that was the reference point, not a confirmed-correct
value. The survey measures TWIST (differential along the rail) and
deliberately excluded TILT (uniform offset - G32 corrects that every print).
It was never actually checking the front for an absolute height problem, so a
front defect would not contradict it, but a front SHIM VALUE cannot be
inferred from today's vibration data alone either - vibration is confounded
by looseness in a way a static measurement isn't.

